          "type": "turn",
          "question": "How is blood pressure actually measured, and why is more than one reading needed?",
          "answer": "The search results explain why more than one reading is needed, but they do not describe how a reading is taken.\n\n**Why more than one reading matters:** Blood pressure changes all day with activity, stress, pain, caffeine, sleep and the time of day. It is usually lowest during sleep and rises before waking. Because of this, the condition hypertension (persistently raised pressure) is judged on repeated measurements, not one. A single high reading in a clinic is not in itself a diagnosis (*General clinical reference, \"Hypertension (high blood pressure)\"*). The same document notes that hypertension often has no symptoms and is usually found on routine measurement.\n\n**What the results do not cover:** The passages do not explain the measuring method, such as the cuff, arm position, or resting before a reading. They also do not say how many readings are taken or how they are averaged. A clinician or a trusted health resource can explain those details.\n\nIf you have a reading you are worried about, or you have symptoms such as chest pain, severe headache, sudden weakness, or trouble speaking, seek emergency care now rather than relying on this information.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
